```

## Phase 1 — foundation and invariants

Implement first:

- environment separation;
- typed configuration validation;
- webhook verification and idempotency;
- audit events;
- RLS and membership authorization;
- server-owned price calculation;
- order state machine;
- notification outbox;
- error monitoring;
- test fixtures and synthetic accounts.

Do not start with AI recommendations or paid broadcasts before these are reliable.

## Phase 2 — customer ordering

Build and test:

- discovery;
- menu browsing;
- bilingual item search;
- cart per vendor;
- options and modifiers;
- address and landmark capture;
- dual-currency summary;
- explicit confirm;
- order receipt;
- cancellation and substitution;
- tracking.

## Phase 3 — merchant operations

Build and test:

- merchant claim;
- merchant verification levels;
- menu extraction draft;
- merchant WhatsApp order card;
- Utility template fallback;
- merchant portal queue;
- accept/reject timers;
- pause and capacity controls;
- notification redundancy;
- staff roles.

## Phase 4 — driver and zones

Build and test:

- driver verification;
- service-zone readiness;
- dispatch;
- pickup handoff;
- GPS freshness;
- route-aware ETA;
- delivery proof;
- COD reconciliation;
- not-collected and failed-delivery handling.

## Phase 5 — payments

Start with sandbox/test mode and one provider adapter:

- payment-link creation;
- signed return/webhook validation;
- amount/currency matching;
- idempotency;
- refunds;
- reconciliation;
- ledger;
- provider outage handling.

Do not build split payments or stored value until providers and Lebanese counsel approve the model.

## Phase 6 — AI recommendations and campaigns

Only after order and menu data are reliable:

- persistent customer preference profile;
- recommendation explanations;
- allergy-safe behavior;
- campaign audience rules;
- consent/opt-out enforcement;
- frequency caps;
- delivery and conversion attribution;
- human review for high-risk campaigns;
- cost and quality monitoring.

