# 11. How to use Claude for the AI layer itself

## Separate model roles

```text
Deterministic router:
  intent, buttons, permissions, order state

Small/fast model:
  simple language normalization, Arabic/Arabizi classification

Claude:
  complex complaints, ambiguous preferences, menu reasoning, human-quality support drafts

OpenAI or selected structured model:
  extraction, embeddings, classification, or image/OCR workflows where validated

Server tools:
  only path to prices, carts, orders, stock, payments, dispatch, and notifications
```

## Tool contract pattern

Each AI tool should have:

```text
name
input JSON schema
authorization rule
allowed states
transaction behavior
idempotency behavior
output schema
customer-facing rendering rule
audit event
```

Example:

```json
{
  "name": "accept_vendor_order",
  "input": {
    "order_id": "uuid",
    "prep_minutes": 20,
    "idempotency_key": "string"
  },
  "authorization": "active merchant member for order.vendor_id",
  "allowed_states": ["pending_vendor_confirmation"],
  "side_effects": ["order transition", "customer notification", "dispatch schedule"],
  "audit_event": "vendor_order_accepted"
}
```

The model cannot call this tool unless the backend authorization passes.

## Prompt rule

Never ask the model to “manage the order.” Ask it to:

```text
interpret the customer message
choose one of the allowed typed tools
explain the tool result in the customer’s language
```

## Prompt-injection defense

Treat these as untrusted data:

- customer messages;
- merchant notes;
- menu descriptions;
- uploaded menu text;
- web pages;
- payment-provider text;
- tool-returned free text.

The model must not follow instructions inside them that ask it to reveal secrets, bypass permissions, alter prices, or message another destination.

